WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.580
- Supposedly, a car is gonna meet us here,

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.243
a truck is gonna meet us here.

00:00:04.243 --> 00:00:05.585
- [Passenger] Yeah, it
must be these dudes.

00:00:05.585 --> 00:00:06.835
- You think so?

00:00:08.685 --> 00:00:10.200
- [Passenger] They're
goin' so slow through here.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.033
- [Dr. Mike] Is this them?

00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:13.250
(white noise whooshes)
(minimal music)

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.470
- So they say look, you
can consider working,

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:17.930
even if you've been exposed,
as long as you have a mask,

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.930
and by the way, we don't
necessarily have enough masks,

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.260
so within those guidelines
as well, they say,

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.570
bandanas and scarves could
potentially be considered

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:28.790
to be used as well.

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.570
- Use scarves and bandanas?

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:31.908
I'm not gonna let my friends

00:00:31.908 --> 00:00:35.500
that're working on the front
lines, nurses, doctors, CMAs,

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.760
that're seeing, treating
patients, to go and get scarves

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.530
and hope that that somehow
protects them from this virus.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.130
I'm gonna do what I can.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.593
What I can is getting these masks.

00:00:45.593 --> 00:00:46.700
(record scratches)
Yes, I'm gonna take

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.190
to the streets in just a moment,

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:50.460
but first, what masks am I talking about?

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.600
I'm talking about N95 masks.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.770
Not these, not these, these.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:55.840
Why do I want these?

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.960
Long story short, N95 masks
protect medical workers

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.170
by filtering out at least 95%
of particulates in the air.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:03.920
They fit very close to the face

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.980
and actually need to
be fit-tested by staff,

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.050
as opposed to surgical
masks, which hang loose

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:10.040
and don't provide nearly
the same protection.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.390
Okay, so, hospitals are
short on these special masks,

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:16.540
but if they can't track them
down, what hope to I have?

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.760
Well, N95 masks aren't only for doctors.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.360
Construction workers and
painters use them as well.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.240
When it became clear

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.100
that the world was gonna
need lots of N95 masks,

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.890
people started scooping them up quickly,

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.810
thereby inflating the price
by a lot, like 700% a lot.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.690
And while hospitals don't have the ability

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.430
to seek these masks out
and negotiate, I do.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.160
Now this is very polarizing.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.100
Yes, people should not be rewarded

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.570
for profiting off a crisis,

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.730
but desperate times call
for desperate measures,

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.660
and I desperately wanted to
protect my colleagues and I,

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:48.470
which brings us to day one.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.333
Just, (beep) told me you
have 3M masks available?

00:01:52.711 --> 00:01:54.293
- [Person On Phone] 3M
masks, the regular ones,

00:01:54.293 --> 00:01:56.140
in the box with the 8210s, yes.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:00.540
- What's the expiration
date on the N95 3M masks?

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:01.973
- [Person On Phone] 2021.

00:02:01.973 --> 00:02:02.806
- All right, so they're good.

00:02:02.806 --> 00:02:05.103
And the cost of the N95 mask?

00:02:07.784 --> 00:02:09.914
- [Person On Phone] I think it was 7.50.

00:02:09.914 --> 00:02:10.747
- Uh-huh.

00:02:10.747 --> 00:02:11.783
And where are the masks?

00:02:12.916 --> 00:02:14.170
- [Person On Phone] In New Jersey.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:15.800
- Bear, are you coming?

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.110
Gimme five.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.090
High five for masks.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.070
High five for masks, come on, high five.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:24.000
As it happened, Bear
knew something we didn't.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.966
This day was not gonna go as planned.

00:02:25.966 --> 00:02:26.900
(trap music)

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.640
Check out this truck that's
gonna deliver some masks.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.970
20 feet of some horsepower,

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.480
that's gonna get us there safely,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.180
and deliver these masks
to keep our doctors

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.130
and nurses healthy.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.821
Someone scratched it.

00:02:40.821 --> 00:02:41.654
(groans)

00:02:41.654 --> 00:02:42.487
Let's do it.

00:02:42.487 --> 00:02:44.130
Where're we gettin' the pick up from?

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:44.968
Yeah.

00:02:44.968 --> 00:02:46.250
Where's the, where's the wipers,

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.030
there we go, oh high-quality wipers.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:50.170
All right, we've arrived

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:51.730
where we're getting these masks from,

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.430
and I'm not gonna lie, it
feels really strange and shady.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:57.920
Supposedly a car is gonna meet us here,

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.590
a truck is gonna meet us here.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:00.815
- [Passenger] Yeah it must be these dudes.

00:03:00.815 --> 00:03:02.065
- You think so?

00:03:04.023 --> 00:03:05.703
- [Passenger] They're
goin' so slow through here.

00:03:06.624 --> 00:03:09.430
(dramatic music)

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:10.360
- [Dr. Mike] Is this them?

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:11.810
I mean, this has to be them, no?

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.130
- [Passenger] What else would this be?

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.610
- They're wearing masks,
and there's three of them,

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.540
and they're, they have their hazards on.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:18.373
We don't have a sign.

00:03:18.373 --> 00:03:19.470
What's the sign?
(muffled speaking)

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.050
- [Passenger] This, (laughs)
this is, that's a quote

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:23.210
from every drug movie and TV show.

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.101
How do I know it's them?

00:03:25.101 --> 00:03:25.934
You'll know.

00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:26.811
Don't ask question.
- (laughing) You'll know.

00:03:26.811 --> 00:03:27.980
- [Dr. Mike] Will they just come out

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.354
and start throwing masks?

00:03:29.354 --> 00:03:30.846
It looks like they're
gonna make a right turn.

00:03:30.846 --> 00:03:32.605
- [Person On Phone] Okay
so, he said any minute

00:03:32.605 --> 00:03:35.283
you're gonna see a big, huge UPS truck.

00:03:35.283 --> 00:03:37.374
Like you know, the color of a UPS truck.

00:03:37.374 --> 00:03:38.207
- Okay.

00:03:38.207 --> 00:03:39.376
- [Person On Phone] They're
gonna pull, pulling up.

00:03:39.376 --> 00:03:40.259
So as it pulls up,
- Okay perfect.

00:03:40.259 --> 00:03:41.092
- [Person On Phone]
you're already gonna see

00:03:41.092 --> 00:03:41.925
the merchandise.

00:03:41.925 --> 00:03:43.158
But he just called the,
- Awesome.

00:03:43.158 --> 00:03:44.116
- [Person On Phone] he called the driver,

00:03:44.116 --> 00:03:45.280
the driver said he's the next stop.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:46.740
- Awesome, perfect, thank you.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.210
All right, so it looks like

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.090
we have this quote unquote UPS truck

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:51.840
that's about to deliver these masks.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.640
I don't know if it's
it, but I hope it's it,

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.980
'cause we've been waiting
here for a few hours now,

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.770
and I desperately wanna get these masks

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.040
to where they can be useful.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.220
Welp, it pulled over, that's,
(laughs) not the thing.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.053
Damn it.

00:04:02.053 --> 00:04:03.330
Think this is the winner,

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.123
I don't think so 'cause
he's not slowing down.

00:04:09.407 --> 00:04:11.060
(laughs) Damn it.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:14.020
All right, so it's like hour
three, waiting for these masks.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:18.070
I hope this is not a fiction,
fictional mask situation,

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:22.350
because I've already paid
$15,000 deposit to get them,

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:23.870
but we can't locate the truck driver.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:27.590
It was in fact a fictional
mask situation, sort of.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.580
The truck never showed up and another guy,

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.160
partner of initial guy,
showed up and informed us

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.380
that guy number one screwed
up the delivery date.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.213
As it happens,

00:04:35.213 --> 00:04:37.500
the shipment was due to
arrive the following day,

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:38.730
at a different location.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.510
Anyway, they apologized profusely

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:43.230
and we made arrangements
to meet on day two.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:46.730
I'm driving now to a fourth
location to get these masks,

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.610
and we're not gonna get the
full amount that we required,

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.250
but at least we're getting
some, fingers crossed,

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.390
I'm getting really frustrated
already at this point.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.960
The goal is at least to get
half the masks today and then,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.580
figure out what we're
doing the rest of the week,

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.930
but that will at least get
us through a couple of weeks

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:01.861
of testing.

00:05:01.861 --> 00:05:03.640
(chimes music)
- Two hours later.

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:05.203
- All right, we got the masks!

00:05:05.203 --> 00:05:08.190
Well at least half the
masks, and they look legit,

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.940
they're 3M, so I'm bringing
them to the warehouse right now.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.470
Michael, does getting this
ease some of your strain

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:14.500
with ordering?

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.620
- Of course, everything
we can get our hands on

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.410
would help the situation,
but people need to understand

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:23.410
that these need to be reserved
for the medical community.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:26.890
They are to prevent someone
from catching the virus,

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:29.060
who's dealing with a critical patient.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:29.893
That's it.

00:05:29.893 --> 00:05:31.790
- Hopefully we can get the rest tomorrow.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:36.300
Hopefully, hopefully we
can get the rest tomorrow.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:37.830
Oh, how naive I was.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.220
I was playing right into
the hustler handbook.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.180
They gave me half the merchandise
and a taste of success.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:44.240
I was feeling good.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.760
This thing was starting to turn around.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:48.220
But all of this was just
priming me for the curve balls

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.040
that were to come on day three.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.820
I just saw some doctors
performing tests on patients

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:56.650
just wearing surgical masks.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:00.650
I saw nurses swabbing a patient's
nose, a famous influencer,

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:02.070
wearing a surgical mask.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:03.920
This is putting them at risk.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:05.320
We don't need to do that.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:07.730
First of all, not everybody
needs to be tested,

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:09.000
at least right now,

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.400
because it's not that we're
gonna run out of testing kits,

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.080
the real issue is, the
safety of the providers,

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:16.800
meaning the nurses and
the doctors and the CMAs,

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.140
but then also, the environment.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.060
If they're walking into an
office and they're sick,

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.280
they can be contaminating
everyone around them.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.870
So it's not so much the
shortage of kits anymore,

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:28.400
it's more about getting the
personal protective equipment,

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.810
and figuring out the logistics

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:31.490
of how to conduct these tests.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:32.340
Later today,

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:34.970
we're gonna be showing you
a drive-through testing site

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.680
that's being done right,
and they're gonna be using

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.180
some of the personal protective
equipment I'm buying.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:42.230
So, I'm really excited
to show that to you guys,

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:43.920
but, on the journey now.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.160
All right, we've arrived
at the location (laughs)

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:47.410
to get the masks.

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:48.470
And we're gonna take 'em directly

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:50.630
to the healthcare
providers that need them.

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:53.190
We already got a few offices
lined up, some drive-throughs,

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.510
hospitals, all of that,
(claps) let's make it happen.

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:57.000
As I walked into the warehouse,

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.930
discrepancies in pricing arose,

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:01.650
and I rushed to get guy
number one and guy number two

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:02.483
to talk it through.

00:07:02.483 --> 00:07:05.290
Just to confirm and
replay back everything,

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:06.780
initially you and I discussed,

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.070
you told me the masks are 7.50 a piece.

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:11.860
I asked if you can do $7
a piece, you said yes.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:14.820
Yesterday I paid 7.50 a
piece, which I don't care,

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.660
I paid the extra 50 cents, not a problem.

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:17.929
- [Mask Seller] Why 7.50?

00:07:17.929 --> 00:07:19.848
You told me it was $6,

00:07:19.848 --> 00:07:22.687
that's what I, what I wanted
to charge him, a dollar extra.

00:07:22.687 --> 00:07:23.683
Why 7.50 now?

00:07:23.683 --> 00:07:25.273
- [Person On Phone] It was
7.50, that was the price,

00:07:25.273 --> 00:07:26.969
I told, I told you, I told you it was,

00:07:26.969 --> 00:07:29.420
I told you maybe seven, I never said 7.50.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.500
- [Dr. Mike] Honestly, I was
just no longer comfortable.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:32.890
Luckily, the day prior,

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:36.380
I was able to buy $27,000 worth of masks,

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:37.810
so I jumped back in the van

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:39.470
and headed to one of the testing sites

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:41.730
where my first batch was already in use.

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:42.600
- Hi everybody,

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:45.310
I'm here today to walk you through

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:48.160
what it's like to come to a
drive-through testing site

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:50.360
to be tested for COVID-19.

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:52.220
I know a lot of people are very anxious,

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.300
and I know this is a very scary time,

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:58.050
but these are great sites to
help people get tested safely,

00:07:58.050 --> 00:07:59.280
quickly, and securely,

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.970
and so we wanna alleviate any
sort of anxiety about this

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:04.220
and we're gonna walk you through

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.780
and drive you through
actually what it's like

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:06.730
for a real patient.

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:09.300
Patients come in if they
already have an appointment,

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.460
and they've already had the
order, so they've been screened

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.620
to make sure that they meet
the criteria that are set out

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.760
by the CDC, and the Department
of Health to get the test.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:22.070
We're gonna have Dr. Mike keep
his windows up at all time.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:24.570
Dr. Mike, with your windows up.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:27.013
Are you scheduled to have
an appointment today?

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:31.770
I'm gonna make sure that
Dr. Mike is on our schedule.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.810
Yes, you are, let's wave you through.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:36.720
At this time, the
patient will be held here

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.820
until they make sure that
the nurse in the tent is free

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:42.410
to do the swab, and once they do,

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:44.310
they're gonna say Mike, Dr. Mike,

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:46.240
continue to keep your window up

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.690
until you get to the nurse
at the end of the tent.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.400
Drive straight through
until the end of the tent.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:52.233
The nurse will tell you

00:08:52.233 --> 00:08:53.650
when it's time to roll down your window.

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:57.420
And at this point, the
patient will stop, the nurse

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:00.020
will put on her appropriate
personal protective equipment,

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:01.300
and give the instructions to the patient

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:03.540
to roll down the window
to have the swab done.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.570
- Any recommendations for patients at home

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:07.330
who are maybe having mild symptoms?

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:08.410
Do they need to get tested?

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:10.180
Do asymptomatic patients get tested?

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:11.350
Do we have any guidelines on that?

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.400
- So unfortunately, right now,

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:16.130
given some of our constraints
like we just talked about,

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:19.830
patients who are healthy,
so they're not elderly,

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.750
they don't have any
underlying medical conditions,

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:24.870
and they feel well, they
can care for themselves

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:26.790
and they can eat and drink,

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:29.000
they're really not being
recommended to be tested

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:29.833
at this time.

00:09:29.833 --> 00:09:31.350
For patients who do meet the criteria,

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:34.150
so anything above that,
of course we recommend

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:36.670
that they get tested, and
if they're critically ill,

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.460
of course that they go try
and get emergency services.

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:42.430
- As I just did a little
mock patient example here,

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:43.660
if I was to get the test done,

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:46.050
how long would I expect
to wait for the results?

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:47.500
- So at our testing facility,

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:49.620
the lab we're using takes
about three to four days

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:51.090
for the results to come back.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.900
They are variable, there are some tests

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:54.630
which I know come back within a few hours,

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:57.580
some that take 24 to 48, and
some that take three to four,

00:09:57.580 --> 00:09:58.700
and some that take even longer,

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:02.180
so I think it ranges a
spectrum, but a few days.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:04.060
- All right, this is why
we did this entire journey,

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:05.990
to get personal protection equipment

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:07.760
for those who are on the front lines,

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:09.900
individuals who're gonna
be working a testing site

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:11.060
just like this.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:13.680
People need to get tested,
but doctors, nurses,

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:15.940
all health professionals
need to be protected.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:19.180
That's why I went on this
journey to get these N95 masks.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:20.930
Sites like this are gonna be using them,

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:22.440
hospitals are gonna be using them,

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.010
medical offices are gonna be using them.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:25.880
COVID-19, alert not anxious.

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:27.910
Alert not anxious doesn't just mean alert

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:30.050
with the news reports and information

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:32.450
that's being put out by the CDC, the WHO,

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:35.350
it means also being alert to
what your body symptoms are.

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:37.290
So if you start getting
worsening symptoms,

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.240
please contact a healthcare provider

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.360
before going to the office,
before going to the ER,

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:45.070
so they can guide you on
how to not get people sick

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:45.990
that are around you.

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:48.470
If you have any medical
supplies, or anything

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:50.820
that you think could be
used as medical supplies,

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:54.030
contact your local hospital,
see if they can be of help.

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:57.410
Because those supplies
are truly truly in demand,

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:59.190
and we need them now more than ever.

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:02.550
And please, stop buying
masks for personal use.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.400
I put together a couple playlists for you,

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.350
here and here, check 'em out.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:08.215
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:11:08.215 --> 00:11:10.632
(trap music)

